      "message": "**Issue in `test_get_frame_cache_size_exceeds_buffer`:** You are intentionally bypassing class encapsulation to pollute the cache using a `reinterpret_cast`:\n\n```c\nauto *raw_storage \u003d\n\treinterpret_cast\u003cstd::array\u003cuint32_t, FP_CAPTURE_TYPE_MAX\u003e *\u003e(\n\t\t\u0026global_context.fp_frame_size_cache);\n\n```\n\nWhile standard layout guarantees might make this \"work\" on your current compiler by overlaying the memory, this violates C++ strict aliasing rules and causes Undefined Behavior (UB). It makes the test highly brittle; any future additions to the `fp_frame_size_cache` class (e.g., virtual destructors, new private member variables, or changes to access specifiers) will silently break this memory overlay and corrupt the test environment.\n\n**Recommendation:** Do not rely on pointer casting to access private class members. Instead, modify the `fp_frame_size_cache` class to expose a safe, test-only setter. You can wrap this in an `#ifdef` block within the header to ensure it doesn\u0027t leak into production firmware:\n\n```c\n#ifdef CONFIG_ZTEST\nvoid set_frame_size_for_test(uint32_t capture_type, uint32_t size) {\n    // safe assignment logic\n}\n#endif\n\n```",
